export const RATIONALE_HTML = "<h1>The Platform Selection Rationale</h1>\n<p><em>Common Ground \u2014 the public record of how the Platform is chosen, and why each inclusion or exclusion was decided. Last updated November 6, 2026. A living page: each review adds dated entries rather than rewriting what came before. The detailed internal selection materials stay internal; this page is the public account of the reasoning.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The promise this page keeps.</strong> Our Governance commitments say that every consequential decision \u2014 which policies qualify, which are selected, how officials are scored \u2014 is documented and published, with \"the reasoning available, not just the conclusion.\" The Platform page shows each policy's support, party split, and source; the full set of findings is browsable in the Look-Up tool. This page is where the <em>reasoning</em> behind those decisions lives \u2014 why each plank is on the Platform, and why qualifying policies were or weren't included.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>How the Platform is chosen</h2>\n<p>Two separate steps sit behind the Platform, and keeping them apart is what makes the result trustworthy.</p>\n<p><strong>First, what qualifies.</strong> A policy enters the candidate pool when it meets all three eligibility criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Supermajority support:</strong> at least two-thirds (67%) of Americans overall.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-partisan agreement:</strong> that two-thirds support holds <em>within both parties</em> \u2014 among Republicans and among Democrats \u2014 not just in the national total.</li>\n<li><strong>Constitutional-rights compatibility:</strong> the policy stays within the limits the Bill of Rights places on government action. This third criterion is a deliberate check on the first two: rights protected by the Constitution are not subject to majority vote, however large the majority \u2014 the Platform's guard against a supermajority overriding an individual right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Majorities are not always right, especially on questions of individual rights \u2014 which is why this third criterion can keep a widely supported policy off the Platform. What Common Ground publishes complements advocacy for rights that lack majority support; it does not replace it.</p>\n<p>The two support thresholds are fixed and published, not adjusted to produce a desired outcome. Common Ground does not decide what Americans agree on; research does. Two further integrity checks apply before we build on a finding: <strong>cross-survey robustness</strong> \u2014 it holds up across independent surveys (Pew, Gallup, KFF and others), not a single instrument \u2014 and <strong>provenance</strong> \u2014 the figure we show matches the researcher's own published result, in the researcher's own wording.</p>\n<p>Every qualifying policy is shown with its evidence \u2014 on the Platform page, where each policy's support, party split, source, and tier appear, and in the browsable Look-Up tool \u2014 whether or not it ends up on a plank. Citizens can examine the full candidate pool, not just the final selection.</p>\n<p><strong>Second, what makes the Core Platform.</strong> Qualifying is necessary, but it is not what puts a policy on the Platform. From the qualifying pool, policies are grouped \u2014 <em>clustered</em>, not narrowed \u2014 into a small, memorable set of planks (nine at the inaugural Platform) that a citizen can hold in their head and name at a kitchen table. The grouping is Common Ground's editorial act; the findings beneath each plank are the researchers', and every plank can be traced to the specific findings under it. Which qualifying policies become planks is judged on published selection criteria: <strong>importance, national scope</strong> (a clear federal responsibility), <strong>clarity, durability</strong> (support that holds across time and polling sources), <strong>measurability,</strong> and <strong>domain balance</strong> across the set.</p>\n<p>A word on balance, because it is easily misread: balance is a test applied <em>among policies that have already qualified</em> \u2014 a check that the set, taken as a whole, isn't concentrated in one domain or one side's priorities. It is never a way to add a policy that fails a gate, and never a reason to drop one that passes. The gates come first; balance only arranges what has already cleared them.</p>\n<p><strong>Review.</strong> The Platform is reviewed every two years, aligned with the midterms, using these same criteria. Any change carries the same documented rationale as the original selection \u2014 which is what the dated entries below record.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>The inaugural Platform \u2014 the nine planks</h2>\n<p>For each plank below: the idea it carries, the qualifying findings that put it on the Platform (with the party split and the source), and the judgment calls. Every figure traces to the source shown on the Platform page itself; this section explains the reasoning behind what you see there.</p>\n<p>Four labels recur, and they are worth defining once:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In Platform</strong> \u2014 more than two-thirds support among both Republicans and Democrats.</li>\n<li><strong>Bipartisan majority</strong> \u2014 over half in both parties but under two-thirds on one side. Congress could add it; it is not on the Platform.</li>\n<li><strong>Candidate</strong> \u2014 bipartisan by reputation, but without a gold-standard independent party split (only advocacy or industry polling). Held off the Platform until a clean figure confirms it.</li>\n<li><strong>Excluded</strong> \u2014 either a partisan wedge (a party under half, or opposed) or a supermajority item deliberately set aside on principle or workability.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1. Government Performance</h3>\n<p><em>Is government serving the people, or itself?</em> The plank is framed around trust, effectiveness, and ethics \u2014 but we state a judgment call plainly: only the <strong>ethics</strong> cluster currently carries findings that clear two-thirds in both parties. Trust and effectiveness name the problem; no policy in those areas is in the qualifying pool yet, so the plank rests on three ethics findings. On the Platform: <strong>ban members of Congress from trading individual stocks</strong> (76%; 78% R, 75% D; PPC 2026 \u2014 passed the House, July 2026); <strong>hold Supreme Court justices to the same ethics rules as other federal judges</strong> (85%; 87% R, 86% D; PPC 2026); and <strong>term limits for Congress</strong> by constitutional amendment (82%; 85% R, 83% D; PPC 2026).</p>\n<h3>2. Money in Politics</h3>\n<p><em>Political power should track votes, not wallets.</em> Eight of twelve candidate policies are on the Platform, strongest on <strong>transparency</strong>, where Republicans back disclosure at 77\u201385%: disclosure of political spending (83%; 85/85; PPC 2026), registering large donors (82%; PPC 2018), and verifying donor identity to block foreign money (79%; PPC 2018). Also on the Platform: <strong>ban foreign money in ballot measures</strong> (80%; PPC 2021 \u2014 passed the House unanimously), a <strong>constitutional amendment to overturn <em>Citizens United</em></strong> (82%; 82/85; PPC 2026), and three \"revolving door\" lobbying limits (71\u201374%; PPC 2022). <strong>Bipartisan majority, not on the Platform:</strong> the public-financing measures \u2014 a small-donor tax credit (67%), 6:1 small-donor matching (60%), and the Stop Act's direct-fundraising ban (55%; 51% R) \u2014 clear half in both parties but not two-thirds.</p>\n<h3>3. Healthcare</h3>\n<p><em>We pay the most and get the least, so the plank aims at value</em> ($14,775 per person vs. $7,860 in peer nations; 79.0 years vs. 82.7). Seven of eight on the Platform: <strong>Medicare drug-price negotiation</strong> (90%; 87% R; enacted, IRA 2022), <strong>capping drug prices to what other developed countries pay</strong> (86%; 90% R), a <strong>$35 insulin cap for everyone</strong> (77%; 70% R \u2014 clearing two-thirds just), <strong>revoking patents on federally funded drugs priced out of reach</strong> (73%), <strong>banning \"pay-to-delay\" generic deals</strong> (76%), <strong>funding substance-abuse treatment</strong> (75%), and <strong>renewing the enhanced ACA subsidies</strong> (81%). <strong>Bipartisan majority:</strong> the $2,000 out-of-pocket drug cap (69%; 58% R) \u2014 already law for Medicare, over half but under two-thirds among Republicans for a broader extension. Abortion sits outside this plank; see the November decision below.</p>\n<h3>4. Immigration &amp; Border</h3>\n<p><em>A system that works \u2014 secure and orderly</em> (a 3.8-million-case court backlog, four-year waits). Six of eight on the Platform, across the whole arc: <strong>more Border Patrol and surveillance</strong> (70%), <strong>deporting non-citizens who commit serious crimes</strong> (85%; 94% R, 79% D), <strong>hiring immigration judges to clear the backlog</strong> (76%; 73% R, 83% D), <strong>E-Verify paired with more work visas</strong> (83%), a <strong>path to citizenship for Dreamers</strong> (83%; 76% R, 90% D), and a <strong>path to legal status for long-term undocumented residents with no criminal record</strong> (77%). <strong>Bipartisan majority:</strong> E-Verify alone, without the visa expansion (68%; 64% R). <strong>Excluded as a wedge:</strong> the border wall (55%; Democrats opposed) \u2014 it fails the two-thirds-in-both test. That the test removes a conservative-coded item here, and progressive-coded ones elsewhere, is the test working.</p>\n<h3>5. An Honest System</h3>\n<p><em>Everyone pays what they actually owe; honest pay for work; honest markets.</em> On the Platform: <strong>closing the carried-interest loophole</strong> (73%; 70% R, 78% D) and <strong>raising the federal minimum wage to $15 over five years</strong> (94%; 90% R, 97% D). <strong>Excluded, set aside on workability:</strong> a wealth tax of 2% over $50M / 3% over $1B (80%; 72% R, 88% D). It clears on popularity but was dropped on policy-design grounds \u2014 capital flight and enforceability make it unlikely to work as intended. This is the clearest case of a supermajority item held off not because it divides the parties but because we don't think it would work. <strong>Bipartisan majority:</strong> raising the corporate rate (63%) and raising taxes over $400k (58%; Pew 2025). <strong>Candidate, held off for lack of a clean party split:</strong> banning junk/hidden fees, right-to-repair, and antitrust action against dominant firms \u2014 bipartisan by reputation, but the only polling is advocacy or industry, so they wait for an independent figure. That is the cross-survey-robustness test in practice.</p>\n<h3>6. Opportunity &amp; Self-Reliance</h3>\n<p><em>A real chance to get ahead, and a floor so a setback isn't ruin.</em> On the Platform: <strong>infrastructure investment</strong> (enacted, 2021), <strong>capping childcare at 7% of family income</strong> (83%; 77% R, 89% D), <strong>protecting Social Security and Medicare from cuts</strong> (79%; AP-NORC 2023), <strong>not cutting Medicaid</strong> (91%; 80% R, 97% D), <strong>funding Social Security by raising the payroll cap above $400k</strong> (79%), and <strong>raising the Social Security minimum benefit</strong> (78%). <strong>Bipartisan majority:</strong> the harder trade-offs \u2014 means-testing the top 20% (65%), raising the retirement age (56%), raising the payroll rate (62%). <strong>Excluded as wedges:</strong> student-debt cancellation and free-college-for-all, which lean Democratic. <strong>Candidate:</strong> apprenticeships and job training \u2014 bipartisan by reputation, no gold-standard split yet.</p>\n<h3>7. Gun Safety</h3>\n<p><em>Keep guns from people who are a danger \u2014 to others, or to themselves.</em> Not guns in general, and not law-abiding owners. On the Platform: <strong>universal background checks</strong> (81%; 70% R, 92% D; Pew 2021), <strong>barring firearms for those under a domestic-violence restraining order</strong> (82%; enacted, 2022), <strong>keeping guns from the adjudicated dangerously mentally ill</strong> (88%; 88% R, 89% D; Pew 2023), and <strong>red-flag laws</strong> (77%; 66% R, 90% D). <strong>Candidate, no confirmed party split:</strong> safe storage (74% overall, but gun-owner support sits at 62% and no published R/D figure) and a mandatory waiting period (no gold-standard R/D figure) \u2014 both wait for a clean number. <strong>Excluded, set aside on principle:</strong> raising the purchase age to 21 (79%; 69% R, 90% D) \u2014 it clears the bar, but 18 is the age of adult responsibility everywhere else (voting, contracts, military service), so a categorical 21 cutoff reads as an age line, not a dangerousness screen. Congress could still adopt it. <strong>Excluded as wedges:</strong> an assault-weapons ban (52%; 27% R; Gallup 2024) and a national gun registry. Support holds across the dangerous-person question and falls off only at the product question.</p>\n<h3>8. Secure &amp; Fair Elections</h3>\n<p><em>Run-the-election mechanics both sides can trust.</em> On the Platform: <strong>photo ID to vote</strong> (83%; 95% R, 71% D; Pew 2025 \u2014 see the November decision below), <strong>paper-ballot backups and post-election audits</strong> (84%), <strong>early in-person voting</strong> (80%), <strong>making Election Day a federal holiday</strong> (74%), <strong>modernizing the Electoral Count Act</strong> (enacted, 2022), and <strong>independent redistricting commissions</strong> (81%; 81% R, 83% D). <strong>Bipartisan majority:</strong> restoring voting rights after a sentence is served (66%; 56% R). <strong>Excluded \u2014 the access-versus-security wedge, in both directions:</strong> no-excuse mail voting (58%; 32% R) and automatic/same-day registration (59%; 42% R) fall short on the Republican side, while voter-roll purges (43%; 24% D) and a ban on ballot collection (45%; 37% D) fall short on the Democratic side; documentary proof-of-citizenship is contested with no clean split. This plank is the clearest proof that the same test excludes each side's preferred measures when they don't command two-thirds in both parties.</p>\n<h3>9. Environment &amp; Energy</h3>\n<p><em>Clean air, land, and water \u2014 stewardship both parties own \u2014 and climate action described concretely rather than sloganized.</em> On the Platform: <strong>making reduced air pollution and greenhouse gases a priority</strong> (86%; 79% R, 94% D), <strong>protecting national parks and public lands</strong> (88%; 88% R, 93% D), <strong>fully funding parks</strong> (enacted, 2020), <strong>US leadership in clean energy</strong> (83%; 73% R), <strong>transition help for fossil-fuel workers</strong> (85%), <strong>energy-efficiency tax credits</strong> (84%), <strong>committing with other countries to cut emissions</strong> (82%), <strong>higher fuel-economy standards</strong> (80%), a <strong>~2%-a-year emissions-reduction goal</strong> (80%), and <strong>tax credits for carbon-free energy</strong> (77%). <strong>Bipartisan majority:</strong> expanding nuclear power (59%; 69% R, 52% D). <strong>Excluded \u2014 framing, not goal:</strong> \"prioritize renewables over fossil fuels\" as a slogan (57%; 28% R) pursues the same end as the concrete items above but loses Republicans in the wording, and the \"mandate\" cluster \u2014 a carbon tax, fossil-fuel bans, gas-car phase-outs \u2014 divides the parties. Here the wording fails the test while the underlying goal passes it.</p>\n<h3>The near-misses, by category</h3>\n<p>Pulling the exclusions together, policies that met the numbers or the reputation but are not on the Platform fall into four groups, and we name which applies in each case above:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bipartisan majority \u2014 Congress could add.</strong> Over half in both parties, under two-thirds on one side: the public-financing measures, the out-of-pocket drug cap, the Social Security trade-offs, felony voting-rights restoration, nuclear power. On the evidence layer, not the Platform.</li>\n<li><strong>Candidate \u2014 awaiting a clean party split.</strong> Bipartisan by reputation but lacking a gold-standard independent figure: junk fees, right-to-repair, antitrust, apprenticeships, safe storage, the waiting period. Held off under the cross-survey-robustness test until confirmed.</li>\n<li><strong>Set aside on principle or workability.</strong> Supermajority items deliberately not carried: the age-21 gun purchase (principle) and the wealth tax (workability).</li>\n<li><strong>Excluded as a wedge.</strong> A party under half, or opposed: the border wall, the assault-weapons ban, mail voting and automatic registration, voter-roll purges and the ballot-collection ban, the renewables-versus-fossil-fuels framing. The Elections and Environment planks show the test cutting in both partisan directions.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2>Decisions from the first two-year review \u2014 November 2026</h2>\n<h3>a. Abortion \u2014 not on the Platform (provisional); kept in the Look-Up tool</h3>\n<p>A survey finding on abortion clears the support thresholds: asked whether it should be a crime to perform an abortion before fetal viability, <strong>84% of Americans say it should not \u2014 74% of Republicans and 91% of Democrats</strong> (Program for Public Consultation, national survey, February 2026; this combines the two options under which abortion is not a crime before viability, which is how the researcher reports the position).</p>\n<p>Clearing the support numbers is necessary but not sufficient. Held to the same tests every policy faces, as currently measured this finding does not yet clear three of them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cross-survey robustness.</strong> The figure exists only in that one survey's specific question. Other major surveys that touch abortion (such as KFF and AP-NORC) ask different questions, so the exact result can't currently be corroborated across instruments. By our published standard, a one-instrument finding isn't yet solid enough to build a plank on.</li>\n<li><strong>Durability.</strong> An earlier deliberative survey on the same topic (2026's figures follow a 2024 deliberative study that offered a 15-week option) put Republican support at <strong>61% \u2014 below the two-thirds bar.</strong> Support that crosses the line between surveys is not yet stable enough to call durable.</li>\n<li><strong>National scope.</strong> Since <em>Dobbs</em>, abortion law is set largely by the states. The Platform's mechanism is to ask your Representative and Senators to act federally, and this finding doesn't fit that federal ask.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>This is the result of the published test, not a judgment that the topic is too contentious.</strong> The Platform already includes hot-button policies that pass the same test \u2014 see voter ID below. We will revisit abortion when new, post-election survey data appears, in particular any non\u2013Program-for-Public-Consultation survey with comparable wording that clears two-thirds in both parties.</p>\n<h3>b. Voter ID \u2014 on the Platform, held to exactly the same test</h3>\n<p>Requiring photo identification to vote is on the Platform, within the Secure &amp; Fair Elections plank. <strong>83% of Americans support it \u2014 95% of Republicans and 71% of Democrats</strong> (Pew Research Center, 2025). Unlike the abortion finding, it is widely replicated across surveys, stable over time, and attached to real federal legislation. It clears every gate.</p>\n<p>Stating abortion and voter ID side by side, under the identical gates, is deliberate. One is often read as a conservative priority and one as a progressive one; both are hot-button; and the same test admits one and holds the other for now. That is the test doing its job, and it is the clearest answer to any charge that the Platform tilts.</p>\n<h3>c. A \"hot-button issues\" tag in the Look-Up tool</h3>\n<p>We are adding a tag \u2014 not a new Platform tier \u2014 that marks findings where supermajorities agree <em>even on the most contested topics</em>, whether or not the policy is on the Platform. The point is honest and striking: agreement reaches further into the hard fights than most people assume.</p>\n<p>To keep the tag from becoming an editorial judgment of our own, it needs an external, citable definition. The working definition: <strong>an issue on which the two major parties' most recent national platforms take opposing positions.</strong> Candidate topics under that definition include guns (background checks, red-flag laws), voter ID, abortion and birth control, several immigration items, policing (body cameras, duty to intervene), and climate and energy.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Counting and wording conventions</h2>\n<p>A few rules we hold ourselves to when describing the Platform and its evidence in public:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Nine planks,\" and which count goes with which claim.</strong> We describe the Platform itself as its nine planks, never as a count of individual policies. When we do cite a count of policies, we keep two figures distinct: <strong>88</strong> is the Program for Public Consultation's own reported number of policies with more than two-thirds support among both Republicans and Democrats \u2014 used when we refer to PPC and its research; <strong>82</strong> is the curated set we maintain in our own Data Library \u2014 used when we refer to the policies we have catalogued. The two are never conflated.</li>\n<li><strong>Combined positions on multiple-choice questions.</strong> Where a survey asked respondents to pick one option from several that nest (for example, stages of pregnancy, or spending thresholds), we report the combined position the researcher publishes, and we show the spread of the underlying options so readers see where the majority comes from \u2014 rather than presenting a single option's share as if it were the whole picture.</li>\n<li><strong>The researcher's wording.</strong> Policies are described as the research worded them \u2014 for example, drug prices \"based on what other developed countries pay,\" and Supreme Court ethics as holding the Court to the same binding standards as other federal judges \u2014 not in a looser paraphrase that could change the meaning.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2>On balance, and a criticism we've received</h2>\n<p>A scholarly reviewer observed, in November 2026, that many of the Platform's policies are ones often associated with Democrats. We take the point seriously, and our answer is on the record.</p>\n<p>The Platform follows the published rule, not partisan balance. Every policy on it has more than two-thirds support among Republicans as well as Democrats \u2014 that is what it means to qualify. We do not add or remove a policy to make the set look even-handed; doing so would break the one promise the Platform rests on. What a policy \"reads as\" politically is not what determines whether it belongs; the cross-party support does.</p>\n<p>Separately, Common Ground does review the <em>examples it leads with</em> in its own writing for balance \u2014 which findings we put first, which we illustrate \u2014 so that how we present the agreement doesn't itself read as coded. That is a choice about presentation. The membership of the Platform is set by the evidence, and only by the evidence.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Candidates for the next review</h2>\n<p>From the Program for Public Consultation's October 2026 battleground survey (figures shown as Democrats / Republicans), these clear two-thirds in both parties and will be assessed at the next review:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Citizen redistricting commissions \u2014 83 / 81</li>\n<li>Congressional term limits \u2014 83 / 85</li>\n<li>The Supreme Court following the same ethics standards as other federal judges \u2014 86 / 87</li>\n<li>Overturning <em>Citizens United</em> \u2014 85 / 82</li>\n<li>Banning stock trading by members of Congress \u2014 75 / 78</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below the bar in the same survey, and therefore not candidates on these numbers: ranked-choice voting (70 / 64) and small-donor tax credits (72 / 63).</p>\n<p>Several of the qualifying items above may already be represented within existing planks; part of the next review is reconciling each against what the Platform already carries, so the same policy isn't counted twice.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>What this page still owes</h2>\n<p>The nine planks above account for the policies on the Platform and for the notable near-misses within each domain. Under our Governance commitment, every <em>remaining</em> qualifying policy that is not on the Platform is also owed a short, documented reason. Where many items share a reason \u2014 \"already covered by an existing plank,\" or \"a state rather than federal matter\" \u2014 we will group them under a shared explanation so the record stays readable, while still accounting for each. Those item-by-item entries, drawn from the full set of qualifying findings in the Look-Up tool, are the next addition to this page.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>This is a living page maintained by Common Ground. Decisions are recorded as dated entries as they are made; the most recent review was November 2026. Last updated November 6, 2026.</em></p>";
